<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippodrome of Constantinople</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hippodrome of Istanbul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hippodrome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>-racing stadium of the Byzantine capital, begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> and enlarged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>. It was the centre of the city's public life and of the Blue and Green factions, and the Nika riots of 532 began there. Its site is now Sultanahmet Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpent-column">Serpent Column</a> and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> still stand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hippodrome-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="Hippodrome of Constantinople" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninara · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultanahmet_Square,_Obelisk_of_Theodosius,_Istanbul_(52121868925).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
